strut\\\" style=\\\"height:2.7em\\\"\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\\\"\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"mord mtight\\\"\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"mord mathnormal mtight\\\" style=\\\"margin-right:0.0077em\\\"\\u003eR\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"vlist-s\\\"\\u003e​\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"vlist-r\\\"\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"vlist\\\" style=\\\"height:0.3558em\\\"\\u003e\\u003cspan\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"mspace\\\" style=\\\"margin-right:0.1667em\\\"\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"mord mathnormal\\\"\\u003eρ\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"mopen\\\"\\u003e(\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"mord mathnormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-right:0.0278em\\\"\\u003er\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"mclose\\\"\\u003e)\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"mord mathnormal\\\"\\u003eA\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"mord mathnormal\\\"\\u003ed\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"mord mathnormal\\\" style=\\\"margin-right:0.0278em\\\"\\u003er\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eSpecific calculations depend on the shape and density information of the situations, but this serves as a general direction to follow when calculating mass. The final concept is inertia, a more blurry concept that cannot be easily be quantified. In Newtonian mechanics, inertia refers to the resistance of the matter toward any external change of the motion state of the matter. For instance, if a car travels fast on highway and needs to stop since there's another parked car on the front road \\u003cspan class=\\\"katex\\\"\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"katex-mathml\\\"\\u003e\\u003cmath xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\\\"\\u003e\\u003csemantics\\u003e\\u003cmrow\\u003e\\u003cmn\\u003e10\\u003c/mn\\u003e\\u003c/mrow\\u003e\\u003cannotation encoding=\\\"application/x-tex\\\"\\u003e10\\u003c/annotation\\u003e\\u003c/semantics\\u003e\\u003c/math\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"katex-html\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"katex-base\\\"\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"katex-strut\\\" style=\\\"height:0.6444em\\\"\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"mord\\\"\\u003e10\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e meters away, the driver needs to stamp the brake so that the car takes a while to slow down. Now consider a new scenario in which a truck is driving on highway, and this time, according to our common knowledge, the truck driver needs to brake the car ealier so that it can stop before hitting the parked car. This reflects the property of inertia that inertia is larger for matter with larger mass; in another word, the motion of matter with larger mass is harder to be changed. Despite that there are no explicit formula describing the relationship between inertia and mass, we believe that inertia is proportional to mass. \\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eIndeed, inertia is one of the physics concepts that haven't been well quantified  (serves more like a premise/assumption based on experimental proof).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eInertia, in quick summary, is the tendency of matter remaining their existing motion state (constant velocity) that also resists external change. To better understand this, see the upcoming page of acceleration, force, and Newton's three laws.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\",\"children\":[],\"parentTitle\":\"Kinematics\",\"parentUrl\":\"/physics/interesting-topics/classical-mechanics/kinematics/\"}");
